 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-* **Noisy Data:** Measurement data (MRI/CT) can cause the seed set construction to produce a large number of seed cells, significantly slowing preprocessing time [S1].
-* **Branch Prediction:** The surface propagation code contains a large number of branches [S1]. Processors cannot readily predict the outcome, leading to poor performance on some hardware [S1].
-* **Holes:** If the volume has holes, boundary cells must be explicitly included in the seed set to ensure connectivity [S1].
+*   **Simplicial Mesh Assumption:** The algorithm assumes a simplicial mesh and PL function, ensuring at most one piece of an isosurface exists in a cell [S2].
+*   **Branch Prediction:** The propagation code contains a large number of branches. Since the processor cannot readily predict the outcome, this can lead to poor performance on modern CPUs [S1].
+*   **Noise:** In noisy data, the seed set construction may fail or become inefficient, leading to many seed cells and slower preprocessing [S1].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### Itoh's Seed Set Method
-Uses local extrema and graph connectivity with thinning [S1]. It sets itself apart by generating a sparse skeleton of the seed set, making it best for smooth data where extrema are well-defined [S1]. Builds on [Canonical Enumeration](1779-canonical-enumeration.md).
-
-### Contour Tree Based Propagation
-Uses van Kreveld's contour tree approach [S1]. It sets itself apart by incorporating saddle points to handle complex topologies, though implementation details are sparse [S1]. Builds on [Canonical Enumeration](1779-canonical-enumeration.md).
-
-### Segment Tree Based Propagation
-Uses Bajaj's set theory and segment tree [S1]. It sets itself apart by organizing seeds for efficient traversal, making it best when complex data structure management is acceptable [S1]. Builds on [Canonical Enumeration](1779-canonical-enumeration.md).
+### Spatio-Temporal Coherency Seeding
+A strategy to find contour seed points for a new frame in animation by examining cells that contained contours in the previously drawn frame. It exploits the fact that contours usually intersect contours of that surface from other viewpoints.
+*   **When to choose:** Interactive visualization of time-varying volume data with continuous viewpoint or isovalue changes.
+*   **Performance:** Adds $O(m)$ time to the extraction algorithm (where $m$ is the number of contour cells), leaving overall running time as $O(m)$ [S3].
+*   **Accuracy:** In animations with continuous change, it extracts an average of 85% of contours (minimum 70%) [S3].
+*   **Pitfalls:** Short segments are more likely to be missed than long ones, but this is not objectionable as short segments are less perceptually important. Missing segments are more likely during quick motion, which is also less perceptually critical [S3].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-* **Time Complexity:** Theoretically near-optimal worst-case time complexity [S1]. However, practical performance is heavily influenced by the efficiency of seed identification and the branch prediction penalty during propagation [S1].
-* **Memory Complexity:** Memory overhead is nearly as much as the NOISE algorithm due to the extrema data structure used [S1]. This is significantly higher than Branch-on-Need Octree (BONO), which retains the original representation with minimal overhead [S1].
-* **Performance:** On the Rage256 dataset, the surface propagation algorithm performed poorly compared to BONO, ISSUE, and NOISE due to branch misprediction [S1]. On the Head 128 dataset, performance was evaluated [S1].
-* **Strengths:** Exploits spatial coherence, handles structured and unstructured meshes, and offers theoretically optimal complexity [S1].
-* **Limitations:** High preprocessing time for noisy data, poor branch prediction performance, and high memory overhead compared to octree methods [S1].
-* **When to Use:** When the data is smooth and has few connected components, and when memory is not the primary constraint [S1].
-* **When Not to Use:** For noisy medical data (MRI/CT) where seed identification becomes expensive, or on hardware with poor branch prediction capabilities [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The propagation step for a set of simplices $S_e$ is $O(|S_e|)$, where $|S_e|$ is the number of simplices in the set [S2]. The total time depends heavily on the seed set construction.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** The seed set method has nearly as much memory overhead as the NOISE algorithm because an extrema data structure is used [S1].
+*   **Performance:** On the Rage256 data set, the surface propagation algorithm performed poorly compared to Branch-on-Need Octree (BONO) and span space methods like NOISE and ISSUE. This is attributed to the large number of unpredictable branches in the propagation code [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Efficient for sparse isosurfaces; avoids visiting unnecessary cells; capable of segmenting single connected components [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Poor branch prediction performance on CPUs; sensitive to noise in seed generation; requires explicit adjacency information.
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced as a geometric decomposition technique that combines aspects of span space algorithms and geometric decomposition [S1]. Key contributors include Itoh et al. [6,7], van Kreveld et al. [16], and Bajaj et al. [1] [S1]. It was evaluated in a case study comparing various 3D isosurface extraction techniques, including Marching Cubes, BONO, NOISE, ISSUE, and Interval Tree [S1]. The method is applicable to both structured and unstructured meshes [S1].
+The method combines aspects of geometric decomposition and span space algorithms. It was notably implemented by Itoh et al., who used local extrema and graph connections; van Kreveld et al., who used contour trees; and Bajaj et al., who used segment trees [S1]. The propagation logic described in [S2] is used for interactive contour tracking in time-varying data, leveraging the Contour Tree to isolate specific topological features.
 
